          "text": "The user is asking about CUDA graphs \u2014 specifically why graph replay can be faster than eager kernel launching, and what the costs/tradeoffs are. This is a technical question about GPU programming, and they want \"a few paragraphs\" of explanation. Let me think through what I know about CUDA graphs deeply.\n\n**Background: What happens during eager kernel launch**\n\nWhen you launch a kernel with `<<<grid, block>>>` or `cudaLaunchKernel`, the CUDA runtime/driver does a fair amount of work on the CPU side:\n1. Argument marshaling \u2014 kernel parameters get packed and copied.\n2. Validation \u2014 the driver validates the launch configuration, checks the function handle, etc.\n3. Scheduling/dispatch \u2014 the launch command is written into a command buffer (pushbuffer) that gets submitted to the GPU.\n4. The GPU's front-end (host interface, GPFIFO processing) then fetches and processes these commands, sets up the grid, and launches it on the SMs.\n\nEach kernel launch has CPU-side overhead \u2014 historically quoted around 5\u201310 \u00b5s per launch (varies by architecture, driver version, whether it's a first launch, etc.). GPU-side there's also launch latency \u2014 the time from the command being processed to the kernel",
